<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-average-deviation">
  <title>Measuring Spread About the Mean</title>

  <objectives xml:id="objectives-average-deviation">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>Calculate the deviations of a data set from its mean.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Explain why the deviations from the mean always add to
        zero.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Calculate the average deviation from the mean and interpret
        it as a typical distance.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Use an interval one average deviation on each side of the
        mean to describe a typical range of values.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Compare the variability of two distributions using range,
        interquartile range, and average deviation.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We now have two complete descriptions of a distribution that do
      not talk to each other. In <xref ref="sec-quartiles-iqr"/> we
      paired the median with the interquartile range: a center based on
      position, and a spread measured about that center. In
      <xref ref="sec-mean-median"/> we built the mean, a center based on
      arithmetic. The mean has no partner yet.
    </p>
    <p>
      This section supplies one. The question it answers is simple to
      ask and surprisingly awkward to answer: <em>how far from the mean
      is a typical observation?</em> The first honest attempt fails
      outright, for a reason worth understanding, and the repair we make
      to rescue it produces a usable measure of spread <mdash/> and
      prepares the ground for the measure statisticians actually use.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-deviations-from-the-mean">
    <title>Deviations from the mean</title>

    <p>
      Consider the sample data set 2, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 9. Its mean is
    </p>

    <md>
      <mrow>\bar{x} = \frac{2 + 2 + 4 + 5 + 6 + 7 + 9}{7}
        = \frac{35}{7} = 5.</mrow>
    </md>

    <figure xml:id="fig-adm-dotplot">
      <caption>The sample data set, with the mean marked at 5.</caption>
      <image xml:id="image-adm-dotplot" source="R-generated/adm-dotplot"
             width="80%">
        <description>
          A dotplot of seven values on a horizontal axis running from 1
          to 10. Two dots are stacked at 2, and single dots sit at 4, 5,
          6, 7, and 9. A dashed vertical line at 5 marks the mean. Some
          values sit almost on the line while others, in particular the
          two 2s and the 9, sit several units away from it, so the data
          are not all equally far from the mean.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Some of these values sit close to the mean and some sit well away
      from it. To describe how far the data are from the mean, we start
      by measuring each distance separately. The <term>deviation</term>
      of a value from the mean is the difference <m>x - \bar{x}</m>. A
      negative deviation places the value to the left of the mean; a
      positive deviation places it to the right.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-adm-deviations">
      <title>Deviations from the mean of 5</title>
      <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>Value <m>x</m></cell>
          <cell>Deviation <m>x - \bar{x}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell>2</cell><cell><m>-3</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>2</cell><cell><m>-3</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>4</cell><cell><m>-1</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>5</cell><cell><m>0</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>6</cell><cell><m>+1</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>7</cell><cell><m>+2</m></cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell>9</cell><cell><m>+4</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>Total</cell><cell><m>0</m></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <figure xml:id="fig-adm-deviations">
      <caption>Each deviation drawn as a distance from the mean. Solid
      segments run left to values below the mean; dashed segments run
      right to values above it.</caption>
      <image xml:id="image-adm-deviations"
             source="R-generated/adm-deviations" width="80%">
        <description>
          The same dotplot of 2, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 9 with a dashed vertical
          line at the mean of 5. A horizontal segment joins each dot to
          that line and is labeled with its signed deviation. The
          segments reaching left to 2, 2, and 4 are solid and labeled
          negative 3, negative 3, and negative 1. The segments reaching
          right to 6, 7, and 9 are dashed and labeled positive 1,
          positive 2, and positive 4. The value 5 sits on the line and
          has no segment. The three negative labels total negative 7 and
          the three positive labels total positive 7.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Now the obvious move is to average the deviations. It does not
      work. The negative deviations total <m>-7</m> and the positive
      deviations total <m>+7</m>, so the sum is zero and so is the
      average. This is not bad luck with this particular data set. As we
      saw in <xref ref="sec-mean-median"/> when we described the mean as
      a balancing point, the deviations from the mean cancel exactly for
      <em>every</em> data set. An average of zero would tell us that
      every distribution has no spread at all, which is plainly false.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-adm-deviation-value">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A data set has mean 12. What is the deviation of the value 7
          from the mean?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>-5</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct. The deviation is
              <m>x - \bar{x} = 7 - 12 = -5</m>. The sign records that 7
              lies five units to the left of the mean.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>+5</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The size is right but the sign is backward. A deviation is
              the value minus the mean, so a value below the mean
              produces a negative deviation. We will drop the signs
              shortly, but not yet.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>19</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This adds the value to the mean. A deviation is a
              difference, because it is meant to measure a distance.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>12</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This is the mean itself. The deviation describes where one
              observation sits relative to the mean, not where the mean
              sits.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="check-adm-sum-zero">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Why can we not measure spread by averaging the deviations from
          the mean?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>The deviations always add to zero, so their average is
            always zero.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct. The mean is the balancing point of the
              distribution, so the negative deviations always offset the
              positive ones exactly. The average would be zero for a
              tightly clustered data set and for a wildly scattered one
              alike.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The deviations are usually too small to be useful.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Size is not the problem. Individual deviations can be
              enormous; the difficulty is that they cancel when added.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The deviations cannot be calculated unless the data are
            in order.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Ordering matters for the median and the quartiles. A
              deviation is a subtraction and can be computed for the
              values in any arrangement.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Averaging requires dividing by <m>n - 1</m>, not
            <m>n</m>.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That divisor will appear in the next section, and it does
              not rescue this calculation. Dividing zero by anything
              still leaves zero.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-average-deviation">
    <title>The average deviation from the mean</title>

    <p>
      We wanted distances, and distances are never negative. The
      cancellation happened only because we kept the signs. So we drop
      them: take the absolute value of each deviation, and average
      those.
    </p>

    <md>
      <mrow>\frac{|{-3}| + |{-3}| + |{-1}| + |0| + |1| + |2| + |4|}{7}
        = \frac{3 + 3 + 1 + 0 + 1 + 2 + 4}{7}
        = \frac{14}{7} = 2.</mrow>
    </md>

    <p>
      This number is the <term>average deviation from the mean</term>,
      abbreviated <term>ADM</term>. In symbols,
    </p>

    <md>
      <mrow>\text{ADM} = \frac{\sum |x - \bar{x}|}{n}.</mrow>
    </md>

    <p>
      Read it literally: an ADM of 2 says that the values of this data
      set sit, on average, 2 units away from the mean. Notice that the
      ADM carries the same units as the data. If these were dollars, the
      ADM would be $2; if they were grams, 2 grams. Notice also that the
      ADM can never be negative, and that it equals zero exactly when
      every value is identical <mdash/> the only case in which no
      observation is any distance from the mean at all.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-adm-quiz">
      <title>Calculating an ADM</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Four quiz scores are 6, 7, 11, and 16. Find the average
          deviation from the mean.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First the mean:
          <m>\bar{x} = (6 + 7 + 11 + 16)/4 = 40/4 = 10</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The deviations are <m>-4</m>, <m>-3</m>, <m>+1</m>, and
          <m>+6</m>, which add to zero as they must. Their absolute
          values are 4, 3, 1, and 6, so
        </p>
        <md>
          <mrow>\text{ADM} = \frac{4 + 3 + 1 + 6}{4}
            = \frac{14}{4} = 3.5.</mrow>
        </md>
        <p>
          A typical quiz score sits about 3.5 points from the mean of
          10.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <exercise xml:id="check-adm-compute">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A data set has mean 20, and the absolute deviations of its
          five values from the mean are 0, 2, 3, 7, and 8. What is the
          ADM?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>4</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct. The absolute deviations total 20, and
              <m>20/5 = 4</m>. A typical value sits 4 units from the
              mean.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>20</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That is the total of the absolute deviations, and also,
              coincidentally, the mean. The ADM divides that total by
              <m>n</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>5</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This divides by 4 rather than by 5. Every value
              contributes a distance, including the one whose distance
              is 0, so divide by the full count.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>0</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Zero is what we get from averaging the <em>signed</em>
              deviations. Taking absolute values first is exactly what
              prevents the cancellation.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="check-adm-negative">
      <statement>
        <p>
          True or false: a data set with a great deal of variability can
          have a negative ADM.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>True</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The ADM averages absolute values, and an absolute value is
              never negative, so the ADM is never negative no matter how
              scattered the data are. The smallest value it can take is
              0, which happens only when every observation equals the
              mean.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>False</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct. Every term in the sum is an absolute value, so
              the ADM is always zero or positive. A large ADM signals a
              lot of variability; a negative one is impossible.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-adm-typical-range">
    <title>A typical range of values about the mean</title>

    <p>
      The IQR gave us more than a number: the interval from <m>Q_1</m>
      to <m>Q_3</m> located the middle half of the data. The ADM does
      something similar for the mean. Mark the mean, then mark one ADM
      below it and one ADM above it. For our sample that interval runs
      from <m>5 - 2 = 3</m> to <m>5 + 2 = 7</m>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-adm-band">
      <caption>The interval one ADM on each side of the mean, from 3
      to 7.</caption>
      <image xml:id="image-adm-band" source="R-generated/adm-band"
             width="80%">
        <description>
          The dotplot of 2, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 9 with a shaded horizontal
          band stretching from 3 to 7, centered on the dashed mean line
          at 5. The values 4, 5, and 6 fall strictly inside the band and
          7 sits on its right edge, while the two dots at 2 and the dot
          at 9 lie outside it. Four of the seven values therefore sit
          within one average deviation of the mean, so the band captures
          a majority of the data.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      This interval is centered at the mean, and its width is set by how
      scattered the data are. Three of the seven values lie strictly
      inside it and a fourth sits on its right endpoint, so the band
      captures the bulk of the distribution. We call it a typical range
      of values about the mean, in the same spirit as the typical range
      from <m>Q_1</m> to <m>Q_3</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The band is also what makes the ADM readable off a graph. Given a
      dotplot or a histogram, locate the mean by eye, then ask how wide
      an interval around it would be needed to catch most of the data.
      Half that width is a serviceable estimate of the ADM. A
      distribution whose values huddle near the mean has a narrow band
      and a small ADM; one whose values sprawl has a wide band and a
      large ADM.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-adm-band-meaning">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A data set has mean 40 and ADM 6. Which statement best
          describes the interval from 34 to 46?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>It is a typical range of values, extending one average
            deviation on each side of the mean.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct. The interval is centered at the mean and its
              half-width is the ADM, so it captures the values sitting a
              typical distance from the center.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>It contains exactly half the observations.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That guarantee belongs to the interval from <m>Q_1</m> to
              <m>Q_3</m>. The ADM band usually holds more than half the
              data, but the exact fraction depends on the distribution.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>It contains every observation in the data set.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Values farther than one ADM from the mean must exist
              whenever the data are not all identical <mdash/> otherwise
              the average distance could not be as large as 6.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>It runs from the minimum to the maximum.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That interval is the range, and it is built from the two
              most extreme observations rather than from the mean and a
              typical distance.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-comparing-spread-measures">
    <title>Three ways to measure variability</title>

    <p>
      We now have three numerical measures of spread: the range, the
      IQR, and the ADM. They answer different questions, and the
      clearest way to see the difference is to put all three to work on
      the same comparison.
    </p>

    <p>
      <xref ref="fig-potassium-children"/> and
      <xref ref="fig-potassium-adult"/> show the potassium content of 76
      breakfast cereals, split into those marketed to children and those
      marketed to adults. Both plots use the same horizontal scale, so
      distances may be compared directly.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-potassium-children">
      <caption>Potassium content of 30 children's cereals, with the mean
      and the ADM band marked. These are not the same cereals sampled in
      earlier sections.</caption>
      <image xml:id="image-potassium-children"
             source="R-generated/potassium-children" width="90%">
        <description>
          A dotplot of potassium content for 30 children's cereals on an
          axis running from 0 to 340 milligrams per serving. The values
          run from 20 to 110 milligrams and are concentrated between 20
          and 65, with a thin scatter of four cereals between 85 and
          110. A dashed vertical line marks the mean near 54 milligrams,
          and a shaded band about 22 milligrams wide on each side of it
          covers roughly 32 to 76 milligrams. Compared with the adult
          cereals plotted on the same scale, these values occupy only
          the left portion of the axis.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-potassium-adult">
      <caption>Potassium content of 46 adult cereals, on the same
      scale.</caption>
      <image xml:id="image-potassium-adult"
             source="R-generated/potassium-adult" width="90%">
        <description>
          A dotplot of potassium content for 46 adult cereals on the
          same axis from 0 to 340 milligrams per serving. The values run
          from 15 to 330 milligrams, with the bulk between 85 and 160
          and a long thin tail reaching to the right through 200, 250,
          and 330. A dashed vertical line marks the mean near 134
          milligrams, and a shaded band about 55 milligrams wide on each
          side of it covers roughly 79 to 189 milligrams. Both the
          overall spread and the shaded band are far wider than for the
          children's cereals.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="ex-potassium-spread">
      <title>Measuring variability in three ways</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which group of cereals is more variable in potassium content?
          Answer using the range, the IQR, and the ADM.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          All three measures agree that the adult cereals are more
          variable, but each one measures something different.
        </p>
        <table xml:id="table-potassium-spread">
          <title>Three measures of spread, in milligrams per
          serving</title>
          <tabular halign="center">
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
              <cell>Measure</cell>
              <cell>Children's</cell>
              <cell>Adult</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Range</cell>
              <cell><m>110 - 20 = 90</m></cell>
              <cell><m>330 - 15 = 315</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><m>Q_1</m>, <m>Q_3</m></cell>
              <cell>35, 65</cell>
              <cell>80, 160</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>IQR</cell>
              <cell><m>65 - 35 = 30</m></cell>
              <cell><m>160 - 80 = 80</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Mean</cell>
              <cell>about 54</cell>
              <cell>about 134</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>ADM</cell>
              <cell>about 22</cell>
              <cell>about 55</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
        <p>
          The <em>range</em> compares the two extremes and nothing else:
          the adult cereals span 315 milligrams against 90. The
          <em>IQR</em> compares the middle halves and pairs with the
          median: 80 milligrams against 30. The <em>ADM</em> compares
          average distances from the mean and pairs with the mean: a
          typical adult cereal sits about 55 milligrams from its group
          mean, while a typical children's cereal sits about 22
          milligrams from its own. An adult cereal is roughly two and a
          half times as unpredictable as a children's cereal by
          whichever yardstick we choose.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <exercise xml:id="check-adm-vs-iqr">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which pairing of a center with a measure of spread is the
          natural one?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>Median with IQR, and mean with ADM.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct. The IQR is built from quartiles, which are
              positions in the ordered data, exactly as the median is.
              The ADM is built from distances to the mean. Each spread
              measure is defined relative to its own center.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Median with ADM, and mean with IQR.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This swaps them. The ADM measures distance from the mean
              specifically, and the quartiles that produce the IQR
              surround the median.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Either center with either spread, since all four describe
            the same distribution.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              They do describe the same distribution, but a measure of
              spread is meaningful only about the center it is defined
              from. Reporting an ADM alongside a median invites the
              reader to combine two numbers that are not about the same
              point.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Mean with range, and median with ADM.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The range is not tied to any center at all; it uses only
              the two extreme values. And the ADM is computed from the
              mean, not the median.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-range-and-adm-disagree">
    <title>When range and ADM disagree</title>

    <p>
      The cereal comparison was comfortable because all three measures
      pointed the same way. They need not. The range and the ADM can
      give opposite answers to the question <q>which data set is more
      variable?</q>, and the reason is worth seeing.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-spread-set-a">
      <caption>Data set A: a small range, but the data sit far from the
      mean.</caption>
      <image xml:id="image-spread-set-a"
             source="R-generated/spread-set-a" width="85%">
        <description>
          A dotplot of ten values on an axis from 0 to 200. Four dots
          are stacked at 0, single dots sit at 45 and 55, and four dots
          are stacked at 100. The distribution is U-shaped, with the
          data piled at the two ends and almost nothing in the middle. A
          dashed line marks the mean at 50, and a shaded band 41 units
          wide on each side runs from 9 to 91, containing only the two
          middle values. The overall range is 100.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-spread-set-b">
      <caption>Data set B: a large range, but the data cluster tightly
      at the mean.</caption>
      <image xml:id="image-spread-set-b"
             source="R-generated/spread-set-b" width="85%">
        <description>
          A dotplot of ten values on the same axis from 0 to 200. Eight
          values cluster tightly between 92 and 108, with a single
          isolated value at 0 and another at 200. A dashed line marks
          the mean at 100, and a shaded band 23 units wide on each side
          runs from 77 to 123, containing all eight of the clustered
          values. The overall range is 200, twice that of data set A,
          yet almost all the data lie close to the mean.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="ex-range-adm-conflict">
      <title>Comparing range and ADM</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Data set A has range 100 and ADM 41. Data set B has range 200
          and ADM 23. Which data set has more variability?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The answer depends on what we mean by variability, which is
          precisely the point.
        </p>
        <p>
          By the <em>range</em>, data set B wins: its values span 200
          units against A's 100. But the range is built from two
          observations, and in B those two are a lone 0 and a lone 200.
          The other eight values huddle between 92 and 108.
        </p>
        <p>
          By the <em>ADM</em>, data set A wins, and by a wide margin: 41
          against 23. Almost every value in A sits about 50 units from
          the mean, because the data are piled at the two ends with
          nothing in between. Almost every value in B sits within a
          handful of units of the mean. A typical observation in A
          really is much farther from its center than a typical
          observation in B.
        </p>
        <p>
          The range reports how far apart the two extremes are. The ADM
          reports how far a typical value is from the center. When the
          extremes are unrepresentative, those two questions have
          different answers.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <exercise xml:id="check-adm-range-conflict">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A data set has a large range but a small ADM. What does this
          combination most likely indicate?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>Most values cluster near the mean, with one or two far
            from it.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct. A couple of distant values stretch the range
              without pulling up the average distance much, because the
              average is taken over every observation and most of them
              are close in.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The values are evenly spread from the minimum to the
            maximum.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              An even spread across a wide range would place many values
              far from the center, which produces a large ADM, not a
              small one.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The data are piled at the two ends with a gap in the
            middle.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That shape produces a large ADM, since nearly every value
              sits far from the center. It is data set A in
              <xref ref="ex-range-adm-conflict"/>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>A calculation error has been made, since the range and
            the ADM must agree.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              They are free to disagree. They summarize different
              features, which is why reporting only one of them can
              mislead.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="check-adm-interpret">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Two classes take the same exam. Class A has mean 74 and ADM 4;
          class B has mean 74 and ADM 15. In two or three sentences,
          describe the difference between these classes, and state what
          the two ADMs do <em>not</em> tell you.
        </p>
        <response/>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The two classes performed the same on average, but class B is
          far less consistent: a typical student in class A scored about
          4 points from 74, roughly between 70 and 78, while a typical
          student in class B scored about 15 points away, roughly
          between 59 and 89. The ADMs say nothing about shape, so class
          B could be uniformly scattered or could consist of two
          separate groups, one strong and one struggling. They also say
          nothing about any individual score, and nothing about whether
          a single extreme result is responsible for class B's larger
          value.
        </p>
        <p>
          An adequate answer states that both classes have the same
          center but that class B is more variable, and uses the ADM to
          describe a typical distance. A strong answer also builds the
          typical range for each class from mean and ADM, and names at
          least one feature the ADM cannot reveal <mdash/> shape,
          individual values, or the influence of a single extreme
          observation.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <conclusion xml:id="summary-average-deviation">
    <p>
      The deviation of a value from the mean is <m>x - \bar{x}</m>.
      Deviations cannot simply be averaged, because they add to zero for
      every data set. Taking absolute values first removes the
      cancellation and produces the average deviation from the mean,
      <m>\text{ADM} = \sum |x - \bar{x}| \, / \, n</m>, which measures
      how far a typical observation sits from the mean and carries the
      same units as the data.
    </p>
    <p>
      Marking one ADM on each side of the mean produces a typical range
      of values about the mean, the counterpart of the interval from
      <m>Q_1</m> to <m>Q_3</m>. The ADM pairs with the mean just as the
      IQR pairs with the median, and reading the width of that band off
      a graph gives a usable estimate of the ADM without any arithmetic.
    </p>
    <p>
      Range, IQR, and ADM measure different things and can disagree. The
      range depends on two observations, the IQR on the middle half, and
      the ADM on the distance of every observation from the center. A
      large range with a small ADM is the signature of a tight cluster
      with a stray value or two.
    </p>
    <p>
      In practice the ADM itself is rarely reported. Its importance is
      that the measure statisticians do use, the standard deviation,
      behaves almost exactly like it <mdash/> which is the subject of
      the next section.
    </p>
    <p>
      Adapted from <em>Concepts in Statistics</em> (SUNY / Lumen
      Learning), CC BY 4.0. The cereal potassium values and the two
      contrasting data sets are reconstructed to reproduce the ranges,
      quartiles, and average deviations published in the source; the
      underlying observations are not supplied there.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</section>
